---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Contact from '../components/Contact.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Research — Mira Bhakta" description="Research on AI-generated content, equity crowdfunding, and investor trust at Cal Poly Pomona.">
<Nav />
<main id="main" class="detail detail--research page-gutter">
<a class="back-link" href="/#research">← Back to home</a>
<header class="detail__header"><p class="eyebrow">Research · Cal Poly Pomona</p><h1>AI, capital,<br /><em>and investor trust.</em></h1><p class="detail__lead">Studying how startups communicate with investors, raise capital, and perform over time—and where AI-generated content fits into that relationship.</p><p class="detail__status">NCUR · RSCA · CARS · 2026 presentations</p></header>


<section class="research-study" id="research" aria-labelledby="research-title">
<div class="research__body">
    <div class="research__study">
      <h2 class="research__study-title" id="research-title">
        Examining AI-Generated Content in Equity Crowdfunding: Implications for
        Investor Evaluation and Trust
      </h2>
      <p class="research__study-meta">
        Cal Poly Pomona · Faculty mentor: Dr. Denis Iurchenko.
      </p>
      <p class="research__study-meta">
        Dataset developed in collaboration with Ruiyang Wang.
      </p>
      <p class="research__study-meta research__study-work">
        I co-built and analyzed a dataset of 220 equity crowdfunding campaigns
        across UK and US platforms. Using Python and Excel, I standardized
        financial and campaign variables to examine fundraising performance,
        venture survival, and bankruptcy or insolvency. The study considers
        AI-related language, sustainability positioning, and international
        orientation as signals in how investors evaluate ventures.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="detail__sections">
      <section aria-labelledby="research-presentations">
        <h2 id="research-presentations">Presentations</h2>
        <div class="detail__body">
          <p><strong>National Conference on Undergraduate Research (NCUR) 2026</strong></p>
          <p><strong>Student Research, Scholarship, and Creative Activities Conference (RSCA) 2026 · Cal Poly Pomona</strong></p>
          <p><strong>Creative Activities and Research Symposium (CARS) 2026 · Cal Poly Pomona</strong></p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="research__note" aria-labelledby="research-questions">
<h2 class="research__note-title" id="research-questions">Questions</h2>
<div class="detail__body"><p class="research__note-text">
          Does AI-generated or AI-characteristic language change how investors
          evaluate a venture and how much they trust it? Does sustainability
          messaging behave differently when it is asserted during the campaign
          than when it is adopted after the raise?
        </p></div>
</section>

      <section class="research__note" aria-labelledby="research-methods">
<h2 class="research__note-title" id="research-methods">Methods</h2>
<div class="detail__body"><p class="research__note-text">
          <strong>Baseline.</strong> Crowdcube campaigns from 2017 to 2018,
          tracked to long-run outcomes and analysed with logistic regression,
          reported as odds ratios.
        </p>
        <p class="research__note-text">
          <strong>New data.</strong> A contemporary Republic dataset records
          daily funding and investor counts with campaign updates and discussion
          posts — collected by hand, because the platform does not permit
          automated scraping.
        </p>
        <p class="research__note-text">
          <strong>Text + surveys.</strong> Computational text analysis compares
          AI-characteristic terminology, linguistic patterns, and syntactic
          structures. Investor surveys and founder interviews are designed and
          pending institutional review board approval.
        </p>
        <p class="research__note-text">
          <strong>Data preparation.</strong> Standardized financial and
          campaign variables, cleaned and enriched records, and checked
          corrections against source URLs and company identities. This
          prepared the 220-campaign dataset for text and statistical analysis.
        </p></div>
</section>

      <section class="research__note" aria-labelledby="research-findings">
<h2 class="research__note-title" id="research-findings">
          Preliminary findings
        </h2>
<div class="detail__body"><p class="research__note-text">
          From the pilot analysis of the pre-AI Crowdcube baseline. These are
          associations in the data, not causal effects, and the textual and
          survey phases are still in progress.
        </p>
        <ul class="research__findings">
          <li>
            International orientation during the campaign was associated with a
            higher probability of long-run survival.
          </li>
          <li>
            Sustainability positioning during the campaign was associated with a
            lower probability of long-run survival and a higher probability of
            bankruptcy or insolvency. Becoming sustainable after the raise was
            strongly associated with higher long-run survival.
          </li>
          <li>
            Post-raise sustainability pointed toward lower bankruptcy or
            insolvency risk, but the evidence was statistically inconclusive.
            Acquisition outcomes were recorded, though the number of acquired
            ventures was too small to support reliable interpretation.
          </li>
        </ul></div>
</section>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</main><Contact /></BaseLayout>
